INT. THE CROWN AND ANCHOR TAVERN - 1783 - NIGHT

Low beams. A fire. Tallow smoke. A cracked auction block stands in the middle of the floor, and the room is crowded to the walls.

GILES, 50, the auctioneer, stands on the block with a gavel and a face like wet bread. MARGUERITE, 30, a widow in good black, sits at the front, hands folded. JEB, 20s, a ship's boy, hovers behind her chair. CAPTAIN SILAS, 60, in a salt-stiff coat, lounges near the hearth.

GILES
Lot forty. One brig, the Halcyon, hull sound, rigging poor. Do I hear fifty guineas?

CAPTAIN SILAS
Forty.

MARGUERITE
Sixty.

JEB
Missus, we cannot afford sixty.

CAPTAIN SILAS
Sixty-five.

MARGUERITE
Seventy.

GILES
Seventy from the lady. Do I hear seventy-five?

JEB
Missus!

CAPTAIN SILAS
Seventy-five.

MARGUERITE
Eighty.

GILES
Eighty. Going once.

The room hushes. Silas looks at her across the smoke. She looks back. Neither blinks. He lifts a single finger.

CAPTAIN SILAS
Ninety.

Marguerite stands. She turns to Jeb, takes the purse from his belt, and puts it on the block.

MARGUERITE
That is everything I own, Mr. Giles.

GILES
It's one hundred and twelve.

MARGUERITE
Then it is a hundred and twelve.

Silas laughs, then stops. She has not moved.

GILES
Sold.
